**What**
`README.md` at the repository root is titled `# facet-git-tree` and its first
section is a Cargo feature table. The repository is named `git-store`, and the
actual pitch - the one a conference audience needs - is buried three levels
down at `crates/git-store/README.md`.

**Where**
`README.md:1-9` (title plus the feature table),
`crates/git-store/README.md:1-6` (the real pitch, which is very good).

**Why it matters**
The first fifteen seconds of a repository visit decide whether anyone reads
the second minute. Right now a visitor lands on:

```
# facet-git-tree
*facet.rs format crates for Git tree objects.*
## Cargo Features
| Feature | Default | Description |
```

That answers "what Rust crate is this" and not "why should I care". Meanwhile
`crates/git-store/README.md` opens with exactly the right sentence - "Store
anything in Git - not as a blob of JSON smuggled into a file, but as a real
tree the stock plumbing can read" - and follows it with a demo that lands the
point in eight lines. The good copy exists; it is just filed under the wrong
path.

This matters specifically for the talk. People will scan the repo during and
after the session, from a URL on a slide. They will land on the root. The
sibling projects (`git-anchor`, `git-query`, `git-forge`) will presumably be
scanned the same way, so consistency across the four landing pages is worth
getting right once.

Related gaps at the root: nothing explains the three-crate split or which crate
a reader should start with, and `docs/acdc-requirement.adoc` (a specification
for an unrelated AsciiDoc extension) sits next to `docs/specification.adoc`
with no index distinguishing them.

**Options**
- Option A - make the root `README.md` the `git-store` pitch: one-sentence
  hook, the recipe demo, the `git ls-tree` payoff, then a short "the three
  crates" map linking down. Move the current content to
  `crates/facet-git-tree/README.md` (which does not currently exist) and point
  the crates `readme =` key at it. Trade-off: none that I can see.
- Option B - keep the root as a neutral workspace index that links to all three
  crate READMEs equally. Trade-off: an index page has no pitch, so the visitor
  still has to click before understanding anything.

**Recommendation**
Option A, high confidence, and it is probably an hour of work. The single
highest return-on-effort item in this review: the writing is already done and
is genuinely persuasive, it is simply in the wrong file.
